EN = {
98: "A few days after his death a porter was sent from the department to his lodging, with an order that he appear at once: the chief demanded him; but the porter had to return with nothing, reporting that he could not come any more, and to the question “Why?” he expressed himself in the words: {M:“Well, he's dead, they buried him four days ago.”} In this way the department learned of the death of Akaky Akakievich, and the next day a new clerk was already sitting in his place, much taller, who set down his letters in a handwriting not so upright but far more slanting and askew.",
99: "But who could have imagined that this was not yet all about Akaky Akakievich, that he was destined to live noisily for a few days after his death, as if in reward for a life that no one had noticed? But so it happened, and our poor story unexpectedly takes on a fantastic ending. Rumours suddenly spread through Petersburg that near the Kalinkin Bridge and far beyond, a dead man had begun to appear at night in the form of a clerk, searching for some stolen overcoat and, under the pretext of the stolen overcoat, dragging from everyone's shoulders, regardless of rank and title, every kind of overcoat: with cat fur, with beaver, with wadding, raccoon, fox, bear coats, in a word every sort of fur and hide that men have devised to cover their own.",
100: "One of the department's clerks saw the dead man with his own eyes and at once recognised Akaky Akakievich in him; but this inspired in him such terror that he took to his heels and therefore could not make him out properly, and saw only how the figure from afar shook a finger at him. From all sides complaints came in ceaselessly that backs and shoulders, and if only those of titular councillors, but even those of privy councillors themselves, were exposed to a perfect chill because of the nightly dragging off of overcoats. The police were ordered to catch the dead man at all costs, alive or dead, and to punish him in the most severe manner as an example to others, and in this they all but succeeded.",
101: "Indeed, a watchman of some district in Kiryushkin Lane had already quite caught the dead man by the collar on the very scene of the crime, in the attempt to pull off the frieze coat of some retired musician who in his day had whistled on the flute. Having seized him by the collar, he summoned with his shout two other comrades, whom he charged to hold him, while he himself reached for just one minute into his boot to pull out from there his snuff horn and refresh for a time his nose, which had been frostbitten six times in his life; but the snuff must have been of the kind that even a dead man could not bear. The watchman had no sooner, closing his right nostril with a finger, drawn in half a handful with the left, than the dead man sneezed so violently that he quite splashed the eyes of all three.",
102: "While they raised their fists to wipe them, the dead man's trace vanished, so that they did not even know whether he had really been in their hands. From that time watchmen conceived such a fear of the dead that they were afraid even to seize the living, and only shouted from afar, {M:“Hey, you, go your own way!”} and the dead clerk began to appear even beyond the Kalinkin Bridge, causing no small terror among all timid people. But we have quite abandoned a certain Important Person, who in truth was almost the cause of the fantastic turn of this otherwise perfectly true history. First of all, the duty of justice requires it to be said that the Important Person, soon after the departure of poor Akaky Akakievich, dressed down to shreds, felt something like regret. Compassion was not foreign to him; his heart was open to many good impulses, in spite of the fact that rank very often prevented them from showing themselves.",
103: "As soon as his visiting friend had left his study, he even fell to thinking about poor Akaky Akakievich. And from that time almost every day the pale Akaky Akakievich, who had not withstood the official reprimand, appeared before him. The thought of him troubled him to such a degree that a week later he resolved even to send a clerk to him to find out how he was and whether he could really help him in some way; and when they reported to him that Akaky Akakievich had died suddenly of a fever, he was even struck, heard the reproaches of conscience, and was out of sorts all day.",
104: "Wishing to distract himself somewhat and forget the unpleasant impression, he went to an evening party at the house of one of his friends, where he found respectable company, and what was best of all, everyone there was almost of the same rank, so that he could not be constrained by anything. This had a wonderful effect on his state of mind. He unbent, became pleasant in conversation, amiable; in a word, he spent the evening very agreeably. At supper he drank a couple of glasses of champagne, a remedy which, as is well known, works not badly in the matter of merriment. The champagne gave him a taste for various extraordinary things, namely: he decided not to go home yet, but to call on a lady of his acquaintance, Karolina Ivanovna, a lady, it seems, of German origin, to whom he felt quite friendly relations. It must be said that the Important Person was no longer a young man, a good husband, a respectable father of a family.",
105: "Two sons, one of whom already served in a chancellery, and a pretty sixteen-year-old daughter with a somewhat curved but charming little nose, came every day to kiss his hand, saying: {W:“Bonjour, papa.”} His wife, still a fresh woman and not at all bad-looking, would first let him kiss her hand, and then, turning it over to the other side, kiss his hand. But the Important Person, though quite content with the tenderness of his family life, found it proper to have, for friendly relations, a lady friend in another part of the town. This lady friend was not a bit better or younger than his wife; but such puzzles exist in the world, and it is not our business to judge them.",
106: "So the Important Person went down the stairs, got into his sleigh and said to the coachman: {I:“To Karolina Ivanovna's,”} and himself, wrapping himself very luxuriously in a warm overcoat, remained in that pleasant state than which a Russian can imagine none better, that is, when you think of nothing yourself and meanwhile thoughts creep into your head of their own accord, each more pleasant than the last, not even giving you the trouble of chasing or seeking them. Full of satisfaction, he lightly recalled all the merry moments of the evening he had spent, all the words that had made the small circle roar with laughter; many of them he even repeated in an undertone and found that they were as funny as before, and therefore it was no wonder that he himself chuckled heartily.",
107: "From time to time, however, he was disturbed by a gusty wind which, bursting out suddenly, God knows from where and for what reason, cut right into his face, flinging scraps of snow into it, bellying out his overcoat collar like a sail, or suddenly with unnatural force throwing it over his head and thus causing him endless trouble to scramble out of it. Suddenly the Important Person felt that someone had caught him very tightly by the collar. Turning round, he noticed a man of small stature in an old, worn uniform coat, and not without horror recognised in him Akaky Akakievich. The clerk's face was white as snow and looked quite like a dead man's.",
108: "But the horror of the Important Person surpassed all bounds when he saw the dead man's mouth twist and, breathing on him a terrible smell of the grave, utter these words: {G:“Ah! So here you are at last! At last I have, you know, caught you by the collar! It is your overcoat that I need! You did not bestir yourself about mine, and on top of that you gave me a dressing-down. Now hand over your own!”} The poor Important Person nearly died. However strong his character might be in the chancellery and generally before his inferiors, and though everyone, looking at his manly bearing and figure alone, would say: “Oh, what a character!” here, like very many who have a heroic exterior, he felt such fear that not without reason he even began to fear some sort of seizure. He himself even threw the overcoat off his shoulders as quickly as he could and cried to the coachman in a voice not his own: {I:“Drive home at full speed!”}",
109: "The coachman, hearing the voice which is usually uttered at decisive moments and is even accompanied by something much more effective, tucked his head into his shoulders just in case, brandished his whip and dashed off like an arrow. In a little more than six minutes the Important Person was already at his own front door. Pale, frightened and without his overcoat, instead of going to Karolina Ivanovna he came home, dragged himself somehow to his room and spent the night in great disorder, so that the next morning at tea his daughter said to him plainly: {W:“You are quite pale today, Papa.”} But Papa was silent and said not a word to anyone about what had happened to him, and where he had been, and where he had wanted to go. This incident made a strong impression on him.",
110: "He even began to say far more rarely to his subordinates: “How dare you, do you understand who is before you”; and if he did say it, it was not until he had first heard what the matter was. But still more remarkable is this, that from that time the appearance of the dead clerk quite ceased: evidently the general's overcoat fitted him perfectly across the shoulders; at least no cases were heard of anywhere of overcoats being dragged off anyone. However, many active and anxious people would not calm down at all and said that in the distant parts of the town the dead clerk was still appearing.",
111: "And indeed, one watchman in Kolomna saw with his own eyes a ghost appear from behind a house; but being by nature somewhat feeble, so that once an ordinary full-grown piglet, rushing out of some private house, knocked him off his feet, to the great laughter of the cabmen standing round, from whom he extorted for such mockery a groat apiece for tobacco, so, being feeble, he did not dare to stop it, but followed it in the darkness until at last the ghost suddenly looked round and, stopping, asked: {G:“What do you want?”} and showed such a fist as you will not find even among the living. The watchman said, {M:“Nothing,”} and turned back that same minute. The ghost, however, was by now much taller, wore enormous moustaches, and, directing its steps, as it seemed, towards the Obukhov Bridge, vanished completely in the darkness of the night.",
}
